  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 325 PM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... There remains a (conditional) Slight risk, Level 2 \nof 5, for severe thunderstorms throughout cntl NC through this \nevening, though the greatest threat/highest confidence area will be \nover the Piedmont at least for the next several hours.\n\nThe models indicate a closed mid/upr-level low will develop across \nthe lwr Great Lakes and nrn Middle Atlantic through early Wed, \nwithin a shortwave trough now progressing across that region per \nGOES-E water vapor satellite data. In cyclonic, nwly flow through \nthe associated trough base, at least a couple of lower amplitude \nperturbations, one of which may be convectively-amplified within the \nband of frontal convection now over the lwr OH Valley, are forecast \nto dig across and offshore the Middle Atlantic. An unseasonably \nstrong belt of wnwly/nwly 700-500 mb winds of up to 30-40 kts are \nforecast and already observed in VWP data over the srn Middle \nAtlantic. Meanwhile, 850 mb-centered moisture transport now directed \ninto the cntl Appalachians will develop ewd and across the srn \nMiddle Atlantic through this evening. \n\nAt the surface and in the low-levels, an MLCAPE minimum over cntl NC \nand Southside VA per SPC mesoanalysis data has resulted from \nmid/high-level debris cloudiness and remnant stratiform rain and \nrelated limited diurnal destabilization, and also from the presence \nof somewhat shallower low-level moisture east of the Appalachians, \nindicated by 12Z-observed mean mixing ratios around 13.5 g/kg at \nRNK/GSO versus 16-17 g/kg upstream at BNA/ILN. The swrn periphery of \nlingering stability and inhibition over cntl NC has been marked by \nan outflow and differential heating boundary draped at 19Z from near \nTNB to VUJ. This boundary may retreat slightly newd and into the nw \nNC Piedmont this afternoon, while a synoptic cold front across WV/KY \nwill continue to progress swd/sewd across the srn Middle Atlantic \nand srn Appalachians. \n\nDeep convection will probably continue to develop this afternoon, \nwith scattered coverage between the outflow boundary and the \nsynoptic front and a broken line along the latter, then propagate \ngenerally sewd and  into the NC Piedmont (particularly wrn portions) \nthrough this evening. Shear profiles will be supportive of \nsupercells, particularly where coverage remains scattered and cells \ndiscrete, versus a tendency for bowing segments within the broken \nline of frontal convection. It remains unclear to what degree any \nadditional development may occur over the ne Piedmont and Coastal \nPlain this evening, deeper in the aforementioned stability there and \nwhere the severe threat is even more conditional and uncertain. \nHowever, a lack of cumulus there and presence of stable/billow \nclouds nearby over much of cntl and nrn VA suggest any such threat \nwill be delayed relative to the nearer storms approaching Watch \n#526; and no local extensions to the current Watch or issuance of a \nnew one altogether for locations to its east are imminently \nanticipated.  \n\n\nKey Message 2: Dry through Saturday, then unsettled into next week. \n\nLate Week through Weekend: High pressure shifts from the Upper \nMississippi Valley on Wednesday, settling over the Mid-Atlantic by \nFriday. Expect near to below-average temperatures and fair weather \nthrough Saturday afternoon. The next round of precipitation arrives \nlate Saturday evening into Sunday morning as tropical moisture \ninteracts with a developing stationary boundary.\n\nInto Next Week: Unsettled weather persists through at least mid-week \nas the stationary boundary lingers overhead. Plentiful Gulf and \nAtlantic moisture will tap into this lingering  boundary, bringing \nmultiple rounds of showers and isolated thunderstorms across the \nregion over several days.",
